        "effect_sizes": {
          "RIM_vs_MAL_CORE": {
            "mean_diff": 0.3653684514085128,
            "cohens_d": 0.781633260656824
          },
          "RIM_vs_FAR": {
            "mean_diff": -0.06599878597702637,
            "cohens_d": -0.11800662990133906
          }
        },
        "neighbor_Tcell_enrichment": {
          "global_mean_Tcell": 0.008702920293619743,
          "mean_neighbor_Tcell_MAL_CORE": -0.24656216884493112,
          "difference": -0.25526508913855084,
          "empirical_p_one_sided_greater": 1.0,
          "n_permutations": 1000
        }
      },
      "narrowing_notes": "Global T-cell scores differed strongly across MAL_CORE, RIM, and FAR (Kruskal\u2013Wallis p = 1.80e-150). RIM spots had higher T-cell scores than MAL_CORE (mean difference = 0.37, d = 0.78, FDR-adjusted p = 4.1e-57), but were modestly depleted relative to FAR (mean difference = -0.07, d = -0.12, FDR-adjusted p = 1.9e-07). Neighbor-based enrichment of T cells immediately around malignant cores was not observed (neighbor mean difference = -0.26 vs global, empirical p (Tcell_neighbors > global) = 1.00), arguing against a strong rim of locally elevated T-cell scores tightly hugging malignant cores. Overall this supports T-cell depletion within malignant cores and enrichment in more distant regions, but the canonical rim pattern is only partially realized, suggesting that neighborhood scale or malignant-core thresholds may need refinement."
    },
